{"name":"Daylily Yellow","hex":"#f8f0d2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"daylily","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several perennial plants, of the genus Hemerocallis, that have fleshy roots, grasslike leaves and colourful flowers that bloom for just one day."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/daylily"},{"term":"yellow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of sunflower petals and lemons; the color obtained by mixing green and red light, or by subtracting blue from white light; the color evoked by light of wavelength around 580 nm; one of the three primary colors in subtractive color systems.","The middle light in a set of three traffic lights, the lighting of which indicates that drivers should stop short of the intersection if it is safe to do so."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a yellow hue.","Lacking courage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/yellow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Daylily","blocks":[{"text":"A daylily, day lily or ditch-lily is a flowering plant in the genus Hemerocallis, a member of the family Asphodelaceae, subfamily Hemerocallidoideae, native to Asia. Despite the common name, it is not taxonomically classified in the lily genus. Gardening enthusiasts and horticulturists have long bred Hemerocallis species for their attractive flowers; a select few species of the genus have edible petals, while some are extremely toxic. Thousands of cultivars have been registered by the American Daylily Society, the only internationally recognized registrant according to the International Code of Nomenclature for Cultivated Plants. The plants are perennial, whose common name alludes to its flowers, which typically last about a day."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Hemerocallis_lilioasphodelus.jpg/330px-Hemerocallis_lilioasphodelus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Daylily"},{"title":"Yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow is a color between green and orange on the spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 nm. It is a primary color in subtractive color systems, used in painting or color printing. In the RGB color model, used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. Carotenoids give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, corn, canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases. Sunlight has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg/330px-Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Lilies_at_Block_Island_IMG_1052.JPG/500px-Lilies_at_Block_Island_IMG_1052.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Lilies_at_Block_Island_IMG_1052.JPG/1280px-Lilies_at_Block_Island_IMG_1052.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Lilies_at_Block_Island_IMG_1052.JPG/500px-Lilies_at_Block_Island_IMG_1052.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Daylilies on Block Island, Rhode Island.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALilies_at_Block_Island_IMG_1052.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Hemerocallis_fulva_-_flower_view_02.jpg/500px-Hemerocallis_fulva_-_flower_view_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Hemerocallis_fulva_-_flower_view_02.jpg/500px-Hemerocallis_fulva_-_flower_view_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The orange daylily (Hemerocallis fulva) in China","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHemerocallis_fulva_-_flower_view_02.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Illustration_Hemerocallis_fulva0.jpg/500px-Illustration_Hemerocallis_fulva0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Illustration_Hemerocallis_fulva0.jpg/1280px-Illustration_Hemerocallis_fulva0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Illustration_Hemerocallis_fulva0.jpg/500px-Illustration_Hemerocallis_fulva0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hemerocallis fulva, illustration of 1885","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIllustration_Hemerocallis_fulva0.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Orange_Lily_Lilium_sp_Rain_2000px.jpg/500px-Orange_Lily_Lilium_sp_Rain_2000px.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Orange_Lily_Lilium_sp_Rain_2000px.jpg/1280px-Orange_Lily_Lilium_sp_Rain_2000px.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Orange_Lily_Lilium_sp_Rain_2000px.jpg/500px-Orange_Lily_Lilium_sp_Rain_2000px.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The tawny daylily (Hemerocallis fulva)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOrange_Lily_Lilium_sp_Rain_2000px.jpg"},{"srcset":"ht